<style>
  /* The whole document is night — a parchment band anywhere here would put a lit page between two
     dark ones and break the reading of the archive as one room. The rhythm is therefore carried
     by scale (plate against sheet) and by the ruled thresholds, never by the ground.
     ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     `:global` IS LOAD-BEARING AND THIS RULE IS THE ONLY GROUND THE PAGE HAS. A page's scoped
     <style> appends `[data-astro-cid-…]` to every selector, and `<body>` is rendered by
     BaseLayout, not by this file — so it never carries this page's cid and a bare `.page-obrazy`
     compiles to a rule that cannot match anything. It shipped that way: the archive stood on
     parchment with every line of its copy set in `--paper`, i.e. text in the colour of its own
     background, from the day it launched until 2026-08-08. Nothing catches it — `astro check`
     sees valid CSS and the register audit reads the cascade it owns, not the ground.
     ANY rule here that targets <body> or <html> needs this wrapper. */
  :global(body.page-obrazy) { background: var(--night, #100f0d); color: var(--paper, #f4f1e9); }
  main { display: block; }
  /* The frame's own scroll-lock, named for THIS body class: a blanket `body.image-open` rule
     would reach documents that never mount the island. */
  :global(body.page-obrazy.image-open) { overflow: hidden; }

  .im-wrap { max-width: 1180px; margin: 0 auto; }
  .im-dot { margin: 0 8px; color: rgba(244, 241, 233, .42); }
  .im-label { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; font-family: var(--capitalis); font-size: 10.5px; letter-spacing: .26em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--candle); margin: 0 0 22px; }

  /* ── Head ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     No hero photograph. The page is dozens of photographs long; opening it with one
     more, larger and decorative, would be the one frame here that documents nothing —
     and the first plate arrives one screen down at 904px, which is the opening a hero
     would have been asked to fake. */
  .im-head { padding: clamp(120px, 16vw, 200px) max(28px, 5vw) clamp(64px, 8vw, 104px); }
  .im-title { font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 300; line-height: .98; font-size: clamp(52px, 10vw, 132px); letter-spacing: -.01em; margin: 0; color: var(--paper); }
  .im-lede { margin: clamp(26px, 3.5vw, 40px) 0 0; max-width: 46ch; font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 300; font-size: clamp(18px, 1.8vw, 24px); line-height: 1.56; color: rgba(244, 241, 233, .74); }
  /* Ruled, because this line is the archive's own colophon of itself and a rule that precedes
     its text is the one entrance this site can make that is literally true of the object it
     imitates. Border blanked only under the motion gate, per the LEAD contract. */
  .im-scale { --rule-ink: rgba(244, 241, 233, .2); margin: clamp(30px, 4vw, 44px) 0 0; padding-top: 18px; border-top: 1px solid rgba(244, 241, 233, .2); display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: baseline; font-family: var(--capitalis); font-size: 10.5px; letter-spacing: .22em; text-transform: uppercase; color: rgba(244, 241, 233, .58); }
  :global(html.voct-motion) .im-scale { border-top-color: transparent; }

  /* ── The index ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     The registrum's grammar one step down and standing on a page instead of hanging from a bar:
     a fixed numeral column so five titles align on one left edge, the title, a hairline LEADER
     ruled out to the count, and the Latin hanging indented under the title as that entry's own
     colophon. No silks — a silk is an object slipping through the slot of a book block, and
     there is no slot here; what the register's dyes do on the chrome, the numeral does here, in
     the same place every evening head on this page already puts its candle.
     The block adds NO hairline of its own: `.im-scale` is ruled directly above it and that rule
     opens the index. Inventing a second one to have something to draw is the move the lead
     register exists to make unnecessary (guardrails §5). */
  .im-index { margin: clamp(34px, 4.5vw, 52px) 0 0; }
  /* The cadence is inline `--i` rather than `data-d`, which only reaches 4 — five rows would need
     a fifth step in a vocabulary shared by every page on the site, to serve one. Step 0.09s, the
     interval `data-d` itself counts in and exactly over reveal.ts's 70ms floor, under which two
     onsets fuse into one event. Same construction registrum.css and the nave card use.

     THE BASE IS THE LEDE'S OWN `data-d="2"`, NOT A THIRD BEAT AFTER IT, and that is a measured
     decision rather than a tidy one. The index is ~477px tall and opens ~50px under the fold, so
     only the first two rows are inside the trigger line at load (1440×900) and the last three each
     cross it ALONE, later, on the reader's own scroll. A stagger authored for five rows arriving
     together therefore spends its whole range on rows that have no one to be staggered against —
     it stops being a cadence and becomes latency on a row you just scrolled to. Starting where the
     lede starts keeps the worst lone arrival at 0.54s, inside the ~2.0s a choreography has before
     it leaves the top of the screen (guardrails §5). */
  .im-entry { --reveal-delay: calc(0.18s + var(--i) * 0.09s); display: grid; grid-template-columns: clamp(28px, 2.6vw, 36px) minmax(0, max-content) minmax(18px, 1fr) max-content; align-items: baseline; column-gap: clamp(14px, 1.7vw, 24px); row-gap: 5px; padding: clamp(10px, 1.1vw, 14px) 0; }
  .im-entry:focus-visible { outline: 1px solid var(--candle); outline-offset: 5px; }
  /* Row 1 only, NOT spanning both: a grid item spanning two rows drops out of its row's baseline
     group and is aligned to the row's top instead, which floats a 15px numeral above the shoulder
     of a 28px title. The evening heads can span because their numeral is half the size of their
     title; at index scale it cannot. The empty cell under it is the hanging indent the meta wants
     anyway — the same shape as `.ribbon-meta`'s 32px offset. */
  .im-entry-roman { grid-column: 1; grid-row: 1; font-family: var(--capitalis); font-size: clamp(12.5px, 1.05vw, 15px); letter-spacing: .12em; color: var(--candle); }
  /* `nowrap` is safe at this measure and load-bearing: the leader beside it is baseline-aligned,
     so a title that wrapped would strand the hairline on its first line. The longest string here
     ("Aeternam — Epitafium dla Gazy") measures ~382px at the 28px cap against a 1180px measure —
     the phone rule below is where the constraint actually bites, and it drops the leader instead. */
  .im-entry-title { grid-column: 2; grid-row: 1; font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 300; font-size: clamp(20px, 2.1vw, 28px); line-height: 1.16; color: rgba(244, 241, 233, .92); white-space: nowrap; transition: color .35s var(--ease, ease); }
  /* An empty box's synthesized baseline is its bottom margin edge, so `margin-bottom` alone sets
     the hairline's height above the title's baseline — no vertical alignment to author. The draw
     is a plain transform on this file's clock rather than the lead register, because a leader is
     not a rule (see the markup). It is dyed by `background-color`, never by `opacity`: this span
     sits inside a `.reveal` node whose opacity the ink register owns.

     0.42s, NOT `--rule-in` (0.85s): that token is the clock of a hairline spanning its block's
     whole measure, and this one is bound to a title on one side. Both of the site's other index
     surfaces draw theirs in about this — `.ribbon-thread` 0.38s, `.via-lead` on `--nave-lead`.
     It matters here beyond taste: the pair interval stacks on a row's own delay, so the last row
     would otherwise still be drawing 1.57s after it was reached. */
  .im-entry-lead { grid-column: 3; grid-row: 1; height: 1px; margin-bottom: 6px; background-color: rgba(244, 241, 233, .26); transform-origin: 0 50%; transition: transform .42s var(--ease-rule) calc(var(--reveal-delay, 0s) + .18s), background-color .35s var(--ease, ease); }
  /* Hidden state inside the motion gate, as the registers are: with JS off or motion reduced the
     hairline is simply there, at full length, and nothing waits to draw it. */
  :global(html.voct-motion) .im-entry:not(.is-in) .im-entry-lead { transform: scaleX(0); }
  .im-entry-count { grid-column: 4; grid-row: 1; font-family: var(--capitalis); font-size: 10.5px; letter-spacing: .18em; text-transform: uppercase; color: rgba(244, 241, 233, .5); white-space: nowrap; }
  .im-entry-meta { grid-column: 2 / -1; grid-row: 2; font-size: 10.5px; letter-spacing: .14em; color: rgba(244, 241, 233, .4); }
  .im-entry-meta .lat { color: rgba(244, 241, 233, .6); }
  /* One gesture, the register's own: the name warms to its evening's light and the leader carries
     that light out to the figure. No lift, no frame — this page's link idiom is colour and a
     hairline (`.im-evening-title a`), and an index row is not a control. */
  .im-entry:hover .im-entry-title,
  .im-entry:focus-visible .im-entry-title { color: var(--candle); }
  .im-entry:hover .im-entry-lead,
  .im-entry:focus-visible .im-entry-lead { background-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--candle) 62%, transparent); }

  /* ── The threshold ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Full-bleed and in the evening's own candle, so a station is recognisable here by the same
     light it burns on its page. The rule is drawn on the SECTION, whose top is exactly where the
     register's trigger fires — a rule anchored to a tall block's foot would draw off-screen. */
  /* `scroll-margin-top` clears the fixed chrome (≈84px) with enough left over that the threshold
     hairline — the thing that says which evening this is — lands below the bar rather than under
     it. Every evening is a link target, so an anchor that hides its own rule states nothing. */
  .im-evening { --rule-ink: color-mix(in srgb, var(--candle) 52%, transparent); padding: clamp(60px, 8vw, 104px) max(28px, 5vw) 0; border-top: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--candle) 52%, transparent); scroll-margin-top: 96px; }
  :global(html.voct-motion) .im-evening { border-top-color: transparent; }

  .im-evening-head { display: grid; grid-template-columns: max-content 1fr; gap: 4px clamp(20px, 3vw, 36px); align-items: baseline; margin: 0 0 clamp(40px, 5vw, 64px); }
  .im-evening-roman { grid-row: span 2; font-family: var(--capitalis); font-size: clamp(22px, 2.6vw, 34px); letter-spacing: .1em; color: var(--candle); line-height: 1; }
  .im-evening-title { margin: 0; font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 300; line-height: 1.06; font-size: clamp(30px, 4.4vw, 58px); letter-spacing: -.01em; color: var(--paper); }
  /* The link is the title, as in the landing's register: the reader already wants to press the
     name of the evening, and a second control beside it would make a toolbar of a series head.
     Gold hairline on approach, the site's own link idiom — never a button. */
  .im-evening-title a { color: inherit; border-bottom: 1px solid transparent; padding-bottom: 2px; transition: color .35s var(--ease, ease), border-color .35s var(--ease, ease); }
  .im-evening-title a:hover { color: var(--candle); border-bottom-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--candle) 62%, transparent); }
  .im-evening-meta { grid-column: 2; display: inline-flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: baseline; font-family: var(--capitalis); font-size: 10.5px; letter-spacing: .22em; text-transform: uppercase; color: rgba(244, 241, 233, .52); }
  .im-evening-meta .lat { color: rgba(244, 241, 233, .72); }

  /* ── The run: a plate and a sheet ──────────────────────────────────────────
     The plate column is `--plate-w` and the inscription column is fixed, so the two together
     rule to the sheet's own edges when the plate is a landscape at full measure — and when it
     is a portrait, the whole pair shrinks to the photograph rather than stranding its label
     across empty measure. `minmax(0, …)` on both is what lets them shrink below their ideal
     before the media query takes over. */
  .im-run { margin: 0 0 clamp(64px, 8vw, 112px); }
  .im-plate { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, var(--plate-w)) minmax(0, clamp(168px, 17vw, 232px)); gap: clamp(22px, 3vw, 44px); align-items: end; margin: 0 0 clamp(22px, 3vw, 40px); }
  /* The plate is a grid child, so it must NOT inherit the sheet's flex contract — `--share` is
     unset here and `calc()` on an unset custom property is invalid rather than absent, which
     would leave the box at `auto`. Stated instead of left to that, because the sheet's phone rule
     below reaches this element too. */
  .im-shot--plate { flex: initial; min-width: 0; max-width: 100%; }

  .im-inscription { --rule-ink: rgba(244, 241, 233, .22); display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 9px; padding-top: 16px; border-top: 1px solid rgba(244, 241, 233, .22); }
  :global(html.voct-motion) .im-inscription { border-top-color: transparent; }
  /* A place is set in the serif for the same reason the colophon sets a photographer in it:
     it is somewhere someone stood, not a field of metadata. */
  .im-inscription-place { font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 300; font-size: clamp(19px, 1.6vw, 26px); line-height: 1.24; color: rgba(244, 241, 233, .92); }
  .im-inscription-date { font-family: var(--capitalis); font-size: 10.5px; letter-spacing: .18em; text-transform: uppercase; color: rgba(244, 241, 233, .56); }
  .im-inscription-credit { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 10.5px; letter-spacing: .1em; color: rgba(244, 241, 233, .42); }

  /* Rows hung by HEIGHT, not cut into columns: each shot holds the fraction of the measure its
     own aspect needs at the row height (`--share`, from lib/galleryLayout), so portraits pack
     four to a row where landscapes take three, and a row keeps its proportions at any width.
     Nothing grows — a short last row hangs centred at the height of the rows above it, which is
     what `center` is for. THE CONTRACT — `flex: 0 1 calc(var(--share) * 100%)` + the same
     `max-width` — is stated in lib/galleryLayout's header and is what the concert page's
     `.kd-gallery-grid` declares too. The two are deliberately not one stylesheet: what genuinely
     drifts is the packing, and that already lives in one module; the rest of each gallery is its
     page's own tone. */
  .im-sheet { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; justify-content: center; gap: clamp(20px, 3vw, 40px); }
  .im-shot { margin: 0; min-width: 0; flex: 0 1 calc(var(--share) * 100%); max-width: calc(var(--share) * 100%); }
  /* Bounds the veil to the image rather than to the figure, which is also what keeps the plate's
     inscription out of it — a place and a date are copy, and copy keeps the ink. */
  .im-shot-media { position: relative; display: block; }
  .im-shot :global(img) { width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border: 1px solid rgba(244, 241, 233, .12); box-shadow: 0 40px 90px -46px rgba(0, 0, 0, .9); }

  /* ── Colophon ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Who held the camera, on its own plate at the foot. Names are set in the serif because they
     are people, not metadata. */
  .im-colophon { margin-top: clamp(24px, 4vw, 48px); padding: clamp(72px, 10vw, 128px) max(28px, 5vw) clamp(84px, 12vw, 148px); background: var(--dark, #080807); }
  .im-colophon-list { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 10px clamp(24px, 4vw, 48px); }
  .im-colophon-list li { font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 300; font-size: clamp(19px, 2vw, 26px); color: rgba(244, 241, 233, .9); }
  .im-colophon-note { margin: clamp(28px, 4vw, 40px) 0 0; max-width: 52ch; font-family: var(--sans); font-weight: 300; font-size: clamp(14px, 1.05vw, 15.5px); line-height: 1.7; color: rgba(244, 241, 233, .56); }
  .im-colophon-back { display: inline-block; margin-top: clamp(36px, 5vw, 56px); font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 10.5px; letter-spacing: .22em; text-transform: uppercase; color: rgba(244, 241, 233, .72); border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(244, 241, 233, .28); padding-bottom: 4px; transition: color .35s var(--ease, ease), border-color .35s var(--ease, ease); }
  .im-colophon-back:hover { color: var(--candle); border-bottom-color: var(--candle); }

  /* ── The rail ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     /koncerty's own, in this page's terms. Hidden where the margin it hangs in does not exist. */
  .im-rail { position: fixed; left: max(16px, 2vw); top: 50%; transform: translateY(-50%); z-index: 45; width: 84px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; gap: 14px; opacity: 0; visibility: hidden; pointer-events: none; transition: opacity .7s var(--ease), visibility .7s var(--ease); }
  .im-rail.is-active { opacity: 1; visibility: visible; }
  .im-rail-num { font-family: var(--serif); font-style: italic; font-size: 19px; line-height: 1; color: var(--candle); }
  .im-rail-line { position: relative; width: 1px; height: 96px; background: rgba(244, 241, 233, .16); }
  .im-rail-fill { position: absolute; left: 0; top: 0; width: 100%; height: 0%; background: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--candle), rgba(198, 164, 91, .35)); transition: height .55s var(--ease-slow); }
  .im-rail-date { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 9px; letter-spacing: .18em; text-transform: uppercase; color: rgba(244, 241, 233, .46); white-space: nowrap; }
  /* Hidden until there is margin to hang it in, and the number is arithmetic rather than taste:
     the rail's right edge stands at max(16, 2vw) + 84, the column's left edge at max(28, 5vw)
     plus whatever centring is left once `.im-wrap` caps at 1180. They clear each other at 1440
     and not before — at 1280 the rail reaches 110 against a column starting at 64, i.e. 46px of
     numeral lying on the photograph. /koncerty's own rail hides at 980 and clears a narrower
     measure (1120), so it is a smaller version of the same sum rather than a precedent for this
     number — see docs/web-imagines-spec.md §19. What is shared is the rule: clearance decides. */
  @media (max-width: 1439px) { .im-rail { display: none; } }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { .im-rail-fill { transition: none; } }

  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    /* The inscription drops under its plate: below this width the margin column costs the
       photograph more than the label is worth beside it. */
    .im-plate { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: clamp(16px, 3vw, 22px); }
  }

  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    /* The index gives up its leader here, and that is the honest reading rather than a squeeze:
       the longest title runs ~273px at the 20px floor against a 334px measure on a 390 phone, so
       a leader between it and the figure would be drawn at a few pixels at 390 and at nothing by
       360. What survives is the shape — numeral, name, and the entry's colophon under it — with
       the count moving to the foot of the row as the figure at its right hand, which is the same
       place the nave card's register puts its date. The title may wrap now that nothing is
       baseline-bound to it. */
    .im-entry { grid-template-columns: clamp(24px, 6.5vw, 30px) minmax(0, 1fr) max-content; column-gap: clamp(11px, 3vw, 15px); row-gap: 6px; }
    .im-entry-title { grid-column: 2 / -1; white-space: normal; }
    .im-entry-lead { display: none; }
    .im-entry-count { grid-column: 3; grid-row: 2; justify-self: end; }
    .im-entry-meta { grid-column: 2; }

    .im-evening-head { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 8px; }
    .im-evening-roman { grid-row: auto; }
    .im-evening-meta { grid-column: 1; }
    /* On a phone the aspect has no room to argue: one shot per row, full measure. */
    .im-shot { flex-basis: 100%; max-width: 100%; }
    /* Which would leave the plate and the sheet exactly the same width, i.e. no rhythm at all on
       the case that matters most — this is 17 700px of phone scroll. The plate keeps its rank by
       taking the whole screen instead: it breaks the page's own gutter, and drops the border and
       the shadow, because a frame drawn to the edges is not sitting on the page, it IS the page.
       The sheet stays inside the measure, so the two are still two things.

       `max-width: none` IS THE RULE, not a tidy-up. The negative margins alone do nothing: the
       rule above caps this element at 100% of its grid area, the box is then over-constrained,
       and the browser resolves it by keeping the width and dropping the trailing margin — so the
       plate comes out at exactly the sheet's width, shifted a gutter off the page's own axis.
       That is what shipped, and it measured as "no horizontal overflow" because there is none. */
    .im-shot--plate { margin-inline: calc(-1 * max(28px, 5vw)); max-width: none; }
    .im-shot--plate :global(img) { border: 0; box-shadow: none; }
  }
</style>
